<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ernst Haeckel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1834">1834</a>–<a href="/years/1919">1919</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Ernst Heinrich Philipp August Haeckel</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ernst Haeckel was a German zoologist, naturalist, physician and artist, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam">Potsdam</a>, who was professor of zoology at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-jena">University of Jena</a> from 1862 to 1909. He named thousands of new species, nearly 700 radiolarians on one trip to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, mapped a genealogical tree relating all life forms and coined terms such as ecology, phylum and phylogeny. He promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-darwin">Charles Darwin</a>'s work in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and developed the recapitulation theory, now disproven, which he supported with incorrectly drawn images of human embryos. The illustrations of his Kunstformen der Natur influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/art-nouveau">Art Nouveau</a>, and he also promoted scientific racism and Social Darwinism.</p></div>
